<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aztlan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aztlan is the legendary homeland from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>) and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples were said to have migrated to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Their accounts describe it as an island in a lake somewhere to the north, which the Mexica left in the 12th century, wandering for generations before founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. Its location, if it existed, is unknown, and it has become a symbol of origin in Mexican and Chicano culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aztlan.jpg" alt="Aztlan" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gemelli Careri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gemelli_Careri_Aztec_Map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Aztlán is the mythical home of the Aztec peoples. The word Aztec comes from the Classical Nahuatl aztecah, meaning people from Aztlán. Several ethnohistorical sources of the colonial period mention it. They list different groups as taking part in the migration to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> who later founded Mexico-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> appear in all of them. Mexica histories relate that seven tribes lived in Chicomoztoc, the Place of the Seven Caves: the Xochimilca, Tlahuica, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acolhua">Acolhua</a>, Tlaxcalteca, Tepaneca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalca">Chalca</a> and Mexica. The Olmeca-Xicallanca and Xaltocamecas are also said to come from Aztlán. These tribes left the caves and settled near Aztlán.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The descriptions of Aztlán apparently contradict each other. Some legends describe a paradise, while the Codex Aubin says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> were subject to a tyrannical elite, the Azteca Chicomoztoca, and fled under the guidance of their priest. On the road their god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a> forbade them to call themselves Azteca and told them to be known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>.</p><p>In one version a man heard a bird calling, Go now, and the people followed an idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a> carried by the priests, travelling until they came to a large lake and saw an eagle on a cactus holding a serpent. There they built Tenochtitlán. Cristobal del Castillo says the lake around the island of Aztlán was called Metztliapan, the Lake of the Moon. The meaning of the name is uncertain, and the Crónica Mexicáyotl's explanation of place of egrets is not possible under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> morphology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Historians tend to place Aztlán either in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> or in the Southwestern United States, though whether it was a real or a mythological place is debated. Friar Diego Durán wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> sent an expedition to find it and that it located a place with the features of Aztlán, but he could give no precise location. From the 1960s Mexican intellectuals seriously considered Mexcaltitán de Uribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayarit">Nayarit</a>, an idea that Alfredo Chavero had raised late in the 19th century; the hypothesis is still debated. Some scholars argue that the true location cannot be found because of the conflicting accounts.</p><p>In 1969 the poet Alurista introduced the notion of Aztlán at the National Chicano Youth Liberation Conference in Denver, in a poem that became the preamble to El Plan Espiritual de Aztlán. For some Chicanos it refers to the Mexican territories the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> won in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a> of 1846 to 1848, and many take it as a spiritual guiding force rather than a place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aztlán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the fall of Tula, groups of Nahuatl speakers, known as Chichimecs and later Aztecs, migrated into the Valley of Mexico, among them the Mexica, who according to their tradition came from a place called <b>Aztlan</b> and founded their city, Tenochtitlan, on an island in Lake Texcoco in 1325.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xochimilca were one of the Nahua groups said to have migrated from <b>Aztlan</b> and settled in the southern Valley of Mexico in the 12th or 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/xochimilco">Xochimilco</a></li></ul></section>
